text:
CREATE LOGICAL SOURCE — defines a schema by name.
CREATE PHYSICAL SOURCE — binds an implementation (file, TCP, generator) to a logical source.
CREATE SINK — defines a sink schema and implementation.
SELECT … INTO — the query proper.



// nes-logical-operators/include/Plans/LogicalPlan.hpp
class LogicalPlan {
public:
    explicit LogicalPlan(LogicalOperator rootOperator);
    explicit LogicalPlan(QueryId queryId,
                         std::vector&lt;LogicalOperator&gt; rootOperators);
    [[nodiscard]] QueryId getQueryId() const;
    [[nodiscard]] std::vector&lt;LogicalOperator&gt; getRootOperators() const;
    [[nodiscard]] LogicalPlan withRootOperators(
        const std::vector&lt;LogicalOperator&gt;& operators) const;
};







NoteExample 2.1: the post-bind plan, in text



The bound plan can be visualised as:
SinkLogicalOperator(name=result, schema={demo$value: UINT64})
└── SourceDescriptorLogicalOperator(name=demo, schema={demo$value: UINT64})
Two operators, one parent-child edge. Note the demo$value qualified name: NebulaStream uses $ to separate the source qualifier from the field name. We will see this convention again in Chapter 6.

2.2 2.2 Stage 2: bound plan → optimised plan
The bound logical plan goes into the query optimiser, which lives in nes-query-optimizer/. The entry point is QueryOptimizer::optimize, defined in nes-query-optimizer/src/QueryOptimizer.cpp. The optimiser is a sequence of pure-function rules:
// nes-query-optimizer/src/QueryOptimizer.cpp
LogicalPlan QueryOptimizer::optimize(
    const LogicalPlan& plan,
    const QueryOptimizerConfiguration& defaultQueryOptimization)
{
    DecideJoinTypesRule joinTypeDecider(defaultQueryOptimization.joinStrategy);
    DecideMemoryLayoutRule memoryLayoutDecider;
    constexpr RedundantUnionRemovalRule redundantUnionRemovalRule{};
    constexpr RedundantProjectionRemovalRule redundantProjectionRemovalRule{};
    constexpr TypeInferenceRule typeInferenceRule{};

    auto optimizedPlan = LogicalPlan{plan};
    redundantProjectionRemovalRule.apply(optimizedPlan);
    redundantUnionRemovalRule.apply(optimizedPlan);
    typeInferenceRule.apply(optimizedPlan);
    optimizedPlan = joinTypeDecider.apply(optimizedPlan);
    optimizedPlan = memoryLayoutDecider.apply(optimizedPlan);
    return optimizedPlan;
}
Five rules, applied in sequence. For our demo query:

RedundantProjectionRemoval deletes the identity projection.
RedundantUnionRemoval does nothing (no unions).
TypeInference annotates every operator with its input and output schemas.
DecideJoinTypes does nothing (no joins).
DecideMemoryLayout chooses row vs. columnar — currently always row, with the columnar path stubbed for future work (Chapter 4).

The optimiser is unusually small for a database engine. There is no cost-based optimisation, no plan-space exploration, no statistics. Every rule is a syntactic transformation. The reasoning is pragmatic: streaming queries are typically short and shallow, and the cost of a wrong plan is amortised over the query’s lifetime, not incurred on each request. We will discuss this trade-off in Chapter 9.

2.5 2.5 Stage 5: pipelined plan → compiled plan
The pipelined plan now goes to the JIT. The phase is LowerToCompiledQueryPlanPhase, in nes-query-compiler/private/Phases/LowerToCompiledQueryPlanPhase.hpp. This is the most complex phase in the engine, and Chapter 12 spends its entire length on it. The summary version: each pipeline becomes an ExecutablePipelineStage produced by tracing through Nautilus, which records the pipeline’s operations as a sequence of operations on nautilus::val&lt;&gt; typed values. The resulting trace is lowered through MLIR to LLVM IR and JIT-compiled into a function pointer.
The single configuration option that matters here is the execution mode:
// nes-query-compiler/private/Phases/LowerToCompiledQueryPlanPhase.cpp
nautilus::engine::Options options;
options.setOption("mlir.enableMultithreading", false);
switch (pipelineQueryPlan-&gt;getExecutionMode())
{
    case ExecutionMode::COMPILER:
        options.setOption("engine.Compilation", true);
        break;
    case ExecutionMode::INTERPRETER:
        options.setOption("engine.Compilation", false);
        break;
}
Two modes. COMPILER produces native code, the default. INTERPRETER runs the trace as an interpreter, which is much slower but incomparably easier to debug — it is the mode you flip to when a generated function gives you a wrong answer and you want to see why. We will use INTERPRETER extensively in this book’s reading exercises.
The output of this phase is a CompiledQueryPlan, defined in nes-executable/include/CompiledQueryPlan.hpp. It contains the compiled ExecutablePipelineStages plus fully-resolved SourceDescriptors and SinkDescriptors — everything the worker needs to execute, with no further coordinator round-trip.

// nes-data-types/include/DataTypes/DataType.hpp
struct DataType final {
    enum class Type : uint8_t {
        UINT8, UINT16, UINT32, UINT64,
        INT8, INT16, INT32, INT64,
        FLOAT32, FLOAT64, BOOLEAN, CHAR,
        UNDEFINED, VARSIZED,
    };
    enum class NULLABLE : uint8_t {
        IS_NULLABLE, NOT_NULLABLE
    };
};



No DECIMAL. No fixed-point numbers. If you need them, you put them in a UINT64 and manage the implicit scale yourself. This is a common simplification in streaming engines; precise numerics rarely pay off in IoT or sensor workloads.
No DATE / TIMESTAMP as distinct types. Time is encoded as UINT64 (typically nanoseconds-since-epoch). The WINDOW(creationTS, SIZE 1 SEC) syntax we saw in Chapter 1 takes a field of integer type and treats its values as time-stamps.
No nested types — no struct, no array, no map. A tuple is a flat list of typed fields. If you have a structured payload, you serialise it into a VARSIZED field and parse it inside a UDF.








NoteExample 3.1: from SQL type to C++ type



The DDL CREATE LOGICAL SOURCE demo(value UINT64) produces a Schema with one Field named value of type DataType{Type::UINT64, NULLABLE::NOT_NULLABLE}. At code-generation time, every Record that flows through a pipeline carries value as a nautilus::val&lt;uint64_t&gt;. There is no boxing, no std::variant, no virtual dispatch. The type system is the C++ type system, modulo NULL handling.

3.2 3.2 Schemas and fields
A schema is just a sequence of fields plus a memory-layout hint. The declaration is in nes-data-types/include/DataTypes/Schema.hpp:
// nes-data-types/include/DataTypes/Schema.hpp
struct Field {
    Field() = default;
    Field(std::string name, DataType dataType);
    std::string name;
    DataType dataType;
};
A Schema holds a vector of Fields and exposes a small interface — getFieldByName, getFieldAt, contains, getSize. Schemas support source qualifiers via the ATTRIBUTE_NAME_SEPARATOR character $. The qualified name demo$value distinguishes a field value originating from source demo from a same-named field in another source after a join. We saw this in our Chapter 2 demo: the sink schema result(demo.value UINT64) is shorthand for the qualified field demo$value.






NoteExample 3.2: schema in a two-source join



A query joining sources cars and parking on cars.id = parking.car_id produces, after the join, a single tuple whose schema has both qualifiers visible:
{ cars$id: UINT64, cars$speed: FLOAT64,
  parking$car_id: UINT64, parking$slot: UINT64 }
The qualified names disambiguate; the engine does not need re-naming rules for join outputs because the qualifier is part of the field name.

3.3 3.3 The unit of data flow: TupleBuffer
Operators do not pass tuples one at a time. They pass TupleBuffers — fixed-size memory regions holding a batch of tuples plus metadata.
// nes-memory/include/Runtime/TupleBuffer.hpp
class TupleBuffer {
    // Reference-counted, header-only wrapper around managed memory.
    // Move steals ownership; copy increments refcount.
public:
    [[nodiscard]] TupleBuffer(TupleBuffer&& other) noexcept
        : controlBlock(other.controlBlock),
          ptr(other.ptr),
          size(other.size)
    {
        other.controlBlock = nullptr;
        other.ptr = nullptr;
        other.size = 0;
    }
};
The TupleBuffer is the engine’s universal currency. Sources produce them. Operators consume them, transform them, produce new ones. Sinks consume them and serialise them out. A TupleBuffer’s size is fixed (default 8 KiB; configurable at engine startup) and its layout is dictated by the schema plus the chosen MemoryLayout (row or columnar; see Chapter 4).
The metadata each TupleBuffer carries is the four progress identifiers we will meet in Chapter 7:

OriginId — which source originally produced this buffer.
SequenceNumber — strictly monotonic per origin.
ChunkNumber — distinguishes multiple buffers produced from one logical sequence event (e.g., a large JSON record split across buffers).
Watermark — the event-time timestamp.

These four identifiers are the engine’s progress contract. Every operator can — and many do — reason about them to decide when a window has all its data, when a probe is safe to fire, when a sink can flush.






NoteExample 3.3: a TupleBuffer in flight



When the file source in our demo query reads demo-input.csv, the input formatter produces buffers shaped roughly like:
TupleBuffer {
    origin = OriginId(1)
    sequence = SequenceNumber(0)
    chunk = ChunkNumber(0)
    watermark = Watermark(&lt;now&gt;)
    layout = RowLayout
    schema = Schema{ demo$value: UINT64 }
    capacity = 8192 bytes
    numTuples = 1024
    data = [&lt;8192 bytes containing 1024 little-endian u64 values&gt;]
}
A worker thread receives this as a WorkTask, the compiled pipeline reads tuples sequentially, and the projection (in our case the identity) re-emits them into a new buffer headed for the sink.

// nes-memory/include/Runtime/BufferManager.hpp
class BufferManager final
    : public std::enable_shared_from_this&lt;BufferManager&gt;,
      public BufferRecycler,
      public AbstractBufferProvider {
    static constexpr auto DEFAULT_BUFFER_SIZE = 8 * 1024;
    static constexpr auto DEFAULT_NUMBER_OF_BUFFERS = 1024;
    static constexpr auto DEFAULT_ALIGNMENT = 64;
public:
    static std::shared_ptr&lt;BufferManager&gt; create(
        uint32_t bufferSize = DEFAULT_BUFFER_SIZE,
        uint32_t numOfBuffers = DEFAULT_NUMBER_OF_BUFFERS,
        const std::shared_ptr&lt;std::pmr::memory_resource&gt;& memoryResource
            = std::make_shared&lt;NesDefaultMemoryAllocator&gt;(),
        uint32_t withAlignment = DEFAULT_ALIGNMENT);
};











NoteExample 4.1: a buffer’s life



A worker thread needs a buffer to write the output of a projection. It calls bufferManager.getBufferBlocking(), which:

Pops a buffer pointer from the pool’s folly::MPMCQueue.
Increments the buffer’s reference count from 0 to 1.
Resets the metadata (origin, sequence, etc.) to defaults.
Returns a TupleBuffer wrapper to the caller.

The worker fills the buffer, attaches it to a WorkTask, and sends it onwards. Three operators downstream, the last consumer of the buffer goes out of scope. The TupleBuffer destructor fires, decrements the count to 0, and returns the buffer pointer to the pool’s queue. No delete, no free, no mmap.

4.3 4.3 Memory layout: row vs. columnar
A schema and a buffer size do not determine where the bytes go. A buffer of 1024 tuples each consisting of two UINT64 fields could be laid out as:

Row layout. [(field1, field2), (field1, field2), …]. All fields of a tuple together; tuples adjacent.
Column layout. [field1×1024, field2×1024]. All values of one field together; fields separate.

Column layouts are usually faster for scans that touch a subset of fields (the famous OLAP advantage). Row layouts are usually faster for operations that touch every field of a tuple at once (the OLTP advantage). Streaming workloads land between: aggregations love columnar; per-tuple projections that touch most fields prefer row.
NebulaStream’s optimiser has a phase called DecideMemoryLayoutRule, in nes-query-optimizer/include/Rules/Static/DecideMemoryLayoutRule.hpp. The phase is small:
// nes-query-optimizer/include/Rules/Static/DecideMemoryLayoutRule.hpp
// For now, we use row-layout across all operators.
class DecideMemoryLayoutRule {
public:
    LogicalPlan apply(LogicalPlan plan) const;
};
The current implementation picks row layout for every plan. The phase exists, the infrastructure for columnar layouts is in place, the JIT-level support for both layouts is there — but the optimiser unconditionally chooses row.






WarningCaveat: columnar is a future direction



The decision to default to row layout is documented in the optimiser as deliberate. Columnar support is partly implemented in nes-nautilus/ (the Record interface supports both), and a future optimiser pass will make a per-operator choice. As of the pinned commit, however, “memory layout” is a single decision applied uniformly. Treat the framework as ready for an upgrade rather than the upgrade itself.

CREATE LOGICAL SOURCE demo(value UINT64);


CREATE PHYSICAL SOURCE FOR demo
    TYPE File
    SET('./demo-input.csv' AS `SOURCE`.FILE_PATH,
        'CSV' AS PARSER.`TYPE`);


CREATE SINK result(demo.value UINT64)
    TYPE File
    SET('./demo-output.csv' AS `SINK`.FILE_PATH,
        'CSV' AS `SINK`.OUTPUT_FORMAT);


SELECT value FROM demo INTO result;

5.4 5.4 Statement binding
Parsing produces a tree of AntlrSQLParser::*Context nodes. Binding turns that tree into a typed LogicalPlan. The binder lives in nes-sql-parser/src/StatementBinder.cpp. Its contract is:
// nes-sql-parser/src/StatementBinder.cpp
class StatementBinder::Impl {
    std::shared_ptr&lt;const SourceCatalog&gt; sourceCatalog;
    std::function&lt;LogicalPlan(AntlrSQLParser::QueryContext*)&gt; queryBinder;
public:
    using Literal = std::variant&lt;std::string, int64_t, uint64_t, double, bool&gt;;
    Impl(const std::shared_ptr&lt;const SourceCatalog&gt;& sourceCatalog,
         const std::function&lt;LogicalPlan(AntlrSQLParser::QueryContext*)&gt;&
             queryBinder)
        : sourceCatalog(sourceCatalog), queryBinder(queryBinder) {}
};
Binding does several things:

Resolves names. demo in FROM demo becomes a reference to the logical source registered under that name in the SourceCatalog. An unknown name is a UnknownSourceType error.
Type-checks expressions. A predicate like WHERE speed &gt; 100 is checked: speed must be a numeric field, 100 is an integer literal, &gt; is a binary operator with the right signature.
Constructs operators. Each clause in the query becomes a LogicalOperator. The DAG is assembled bottom-up: source nodes first, then projection/selection/window/join, then sink at the root.
Validates output schemas. The query’s output schema must match the sink’s declared schema. SELECT value FROM demo INTO result requires that result’s schema be compatible with the single-column projection.

The output is a LogicalPlan ready for the optimiser (Chapter 9).






TipReal-system reference: SQL→AST→logical-plan in Calcite



Apache Calcite, the parser used by Flink SQL and Hive, follows a similar shape: parse tree → SqlNode AST → RelNode logical plan, with binding (“validation”) in between. The two-stage approach is ANTLR-style: keep the syntactic tree close to the grammar, do semantic work in a second pass. NebulaStream’s binder is much simpler than Calcite’s because the language is much smaller.

6.2 6.2 The C++20 concept twist
The operator hierarchy is not a virtual class hierarchy. Instead, NebulaStream uses C++20 concepts to define what a logical operator is, then type-erases concrete operators behind a wrapper:
// nes-logical-operators/include/Operators/LogicalOperator.hpp
template &lt;typename T&gt;
concept LogicalOperatorConcept = requires(T t) {
    { t.explain() } -&gt; std::convertible_to&lt;std::string&gt;;
    { t.getChildren() }
        -&gt; std::convertible_to&lt;std::vector&lt;LogicalOperator&gt;&gt;;
    { t.withChildren(std::vector&lt;LogicalOperator&gt;{}) }
        -&gt; std::convertible_to&lt;LogicalOperator&gt;;
    { t.getOutputSchema() }
        -&gt; std::convertible_to&lt;Schema&gt;;
    { t.getInputSchemas() }
        -&gt; std::convertible_to&lt;std::vector&lt;Schema&gt;&gt;;
};
A type satisfies the concept if it has those five methods. LogicalOperator itself is a type-erased reference type: under the hood it stores an any-like container plus a vtable of pointers to those five concept methods, so the cost of dispatch is the same as a virtual call but the concrete operator types never need to inherit from anything.
The benefit is composition. A user-defined operator only needs to satisfy the concept; it does not need to inherit from LogicalOperator. Adding a new operator (say, a hypothetical LimitLogicalOperator from Chapter 5’s exercise) is a class with five methods, not a class deriving from a chain of bases.






TipReal-system reference: how Calcite does this



Apache Calcite uses traditional inheritance: every operator extends RelNode (a 4000-line virtual class) and its rule engine walks a class hierarchy. The result is faster to grow at scale (easier to add framework-wide functionality) but harder to extend from outside (every new operator inherits a lot of implicit contract).
NebulaStream’s concept-based approach is the inverse trade. Easier to extend from outside; needs more discipline at the framework level because the contract is enforced only at use sites, not in a shared base class.

6.3 6.3 The plan as an immutable DAG
A LogicalPlan is a vector of root operators. The DAG is read by walking children:
// nes-logical-operators/include/Plans/LogicalPlan.hpp
class LogicalPlan {
public:
    LogicalPlan() = delete;
    explicit LogicalPlan(LogicalOperator rootOperator);
    explicit LogicalPlan(QueryId queryId,
                         std::vector&lt;LogicalOperator&gt; rootOperators);
    [[nodiscard]] QueryId getQueryId() const;
    [[nodiscard]] std::vector&lt;LogicalOperator&gt; getRootOperators() const;
    [[nodiscard]] LogicalPlan withRootOperators(
        const std::vector&lt;LogicalOperator&gt;& operators) const;
private:
    QueryId queryId = INVALID_QUERY_ID;
    std::vector&lt;LogicalOperator&gt; rootOperators;
    std::string originalSql;
};
The plan is immutable. There is no setRootOperators; instead, withRootOperators returns a new plan. Optimiser rules accept a plan and return a transformed plan; they never mutate in place.
Two consequences fall out of this.
Plans are cheap to share. A plan in mid-optimisation can be reasoned about by multiple rule engines without any defensive copying. Each rule’s output is a different plan; they coexist.
Plans are cheap to inspect. A debugger or logger can dump a plan without worrying about race conditions or visiting an in- progress mutation. We use this in Chapter 9 when we walk through optimiser-rule output.
The cost is allocation: every transformation allocates a new top- level LogicalPlan object, plus any operator nodes that changed. Because operators inside the plan are reference-counted (they share their type-erased payloads through shared_ptr-like pointers), unchanged subtrees are shared. The actual copy cost is small.

6.4 6.4 Stateful operators carry extra metadata
Stateless operators (Projection, Selection, Union) carry only their input and output schemas. Stateful operators carry more.
The WindowedAggregationLogicalOperator is representative:
// nes-logical-operators/include/Operators/Windows/WindowedAggregationLogicalOperator.hpp
class WindowedAggregationLogicalOperator final : public OriginIdAssigner {
public:
    WindowedAggregationLogicalOperator(
        std::vector&lt;FieldAccessLogicalFunction&gt; groupingKey,
        std::vector&lt;std::shared_ptr&lt;WindowAggregationLogicalFunction&gt;&gt;
            aggregationFunctions,
        std::shared_ptr&lt;Windowing::WindowType&gt; windowType);
    [[nodiscard]] bool isKeyed() const;
    [[nodiscard]] std::vector&lt;std::shared_ptr&lt;WindowAggregationLogicalFunction&gt;&gt;
        getWindowAggregation() const;
    [[nodiscard]] std::shared_ptr&lt;Windowing::WindowType&gt; getWindowType() const;
    [[nodiscard]] std::vector&lt;FieldAccessLogicalFunction&gt;
        getGroupingKeys() const;
};
Three pieces of metadata: a grouping key (possibly empty for global aggregation), a list of aggregation functions, and a window type. The window type is a separate Windowing::WindowType hierarchy covering tumbling, sliding, and session windows. Chapter 7 uses this object pervasively when discussing watermark-driven triggering.
OriginIdAssigner is a base mixin: stateful operators create new origins. The output of an aggregation is logically a new stream, with a fresh OriginId; downstream operators reason about its watermarks independently of the input streams. We will see this machinery in Chapter 7.






NoteExample 6.1: a windowed aggregation plan



For SELECT key, AVG(speed) FROM vehicles GROUP BY key WINDOW TUMBLING(creationTS, SIZE 1 SECOND) INTO alerts, the bound plan shape is:
SinkLogicalOperator(alerts)
└── WindowedAggregationLogicalOperator(
        groupingKey={vehicles$key},
        aggregations=[Avg(vehicles$speed)],
        window=TumblingWindow(creationTS, 1s))
    └── SourceDescriptorLogicalOperator(vehicles)
Three operators. Note that SELECT key and SELECT AVG(speed) are not separate Projection nodes — they are absorbed into the aggregation operator’s grouping key and aggregation functions, respectively. This is a small but worth-noting fact: NebulaStream’s algebra does not separate “compute the aggregation” from “project the result”; they are one operator.

7.3 7.3 Watermark advancement
A source emits a watermark per buffer. The watermark is a promise about all future buffers from that source: “I will not produce a tuple with event time less than this watermark.” Once promised, the source is bound to honour it.
Sources use one of two strategies:

Punctuated watermarks. The watermark is set to the maximum event time seen so far minus a slack δ. The slack accommodates out-of-order tuples within a single source.
Periodic watermarks. The watermark is recomputed every N buffers or every fixed interval, with the promise being made for the period as a whole.

NebulaStream’s source implementations use both, configured per source type. The CSV file source, replaying historical data, uses the maximum-seen-event-time strategy because there is no real-world out-of-order; the file is what it is. A TCP source consuming a live feed uses a periodic strategy with a configurable slack.
When a buffer with watermark W arrives at an operator, the operator advances its local notion of “watermark for origin O” to max(current, W). For operators with multiple inputs (joins, unions), the output watermark is the minimum across origins. This minimum-advance is the engine’s analogue of two-phase commit for time: you cannot advance past origin O until O is willing to advance with you.
The component that implements this is MultiOriginWatermarkProcessor, defined in nes-physical-operators/:
// nes-physical-operators/src/Watermark/MultiOriginWatermarkProcessor.cpp (sketch)
Timestamp MultiOriginWatermarkProcessor::updateWatermark(
    Timestamp ts, SequenceData sequenceData, OriginId origin) const
{
    for (size_t originIndex = 0; originIndex &lt; origins.size(); ++originIndex) {
        if (origins[originIndex] == origin) {
            watermarkProcessors[originIndex]-&gt;emplace(sequenceData, ts.getRawValue());
        }
    }
    return getCurrentWatermark();  // returns min across all origins
}
Each origin has its own NonBlockingMonotonicSeqQueue — NebulaStream’s lock-free ordered-queue primitive — that maintains the contiguous-prefix invariant we discussed in Section 7.4. A getCurrentWatermark() call walks the queues, takes each origin’s contiguous-prefix watermark, and returns the minimum. This is the engine’s distributed-friendly progress lattice in 30 lines of code.

7.5 7.5 Chunks and the SequenceShredder
The chunk number handles a different problem: a single logical event whose physical encoding spans multiple buffers. A 30 KiB JSON record cannot fit in an 8 KiB buffer; it produces four buffers with the same SequenceNumber and ChunkNumbers 0, 1, 2, 3, the last one marked isLastChunk = true.
The SequenceShredder (described at length in nes-input-formatters/README.md) is the component that resolves multi-chunk events. It accumulates chunks per sequence number and emits the assembled logical record only when all chunks have arrived and isLastChunk is set. It also drives a small piece of state that downstream operators need:
// from the watermarking design doc
bool seenAllChunks(OriginId origin, SequenceNumber seq,
                   ChunkNumber chunk, bool isLastChunk)
{
    auto& [lastChunk, numSeenChunks]
        = getLastChunkNumberAndNumSeenChunks(seq);
    if (isLastChunk) { lastChunk = chunk; }
    ++numSeenChunks;
    return numSeenChunks == lastChunk + 1;
}
This is the engine’s per-sequence completion check. A window operator that needs to know “have all chunks of sequence S arrived?” calls seenAllChunks. The function is a few lines of state per sequence number; in steady state, almost every sequence has a single chunk and the bookkeeping is cheap.

// nes-sources/include/Sources/Source.hpp
class Source {
public:
    class FillTupleBufferResult {
        struct EoS {};
        struct Data { size_t sizeInBytes; };
        std::variant&lt;EoS, Data&gt; result = EoS{};
    public:
        static FillTupleBufferResult eos() { return {}; }
        static FillTupleBufferResult withBytes(size_t sizeInBytes)
            { return FillTupleBufferResult{sizeInBytes}; }
        [[nodiscard]] bool isEoS() const { return std::holds_alternative&lt;EoS&gt;(result); }
        [[nodiscard]] size_t getNumberOfBytes() const
            { return std::get&lt;Data&gt;(result).sizeInBytes; }
    };
    virtual FillTupleBufferResult fillTupleBuffer(TupleBuffer buffer) = 0;
};

8.2 8.2 The descriptor model
Every source has two artefacts in the engine:

A SourceDescriptor, declared up front by the user and resolved by the binder. It is just configuration: type name (e.g., “TCP”, “File”), key-value config map, schema.
A Source implementation, instantiated at query startup from the descriptor by looking up the type name in the source registry.

The descriptor contains everything the engine needs to construct the source. Critically, this design makes workers stateless with respect to source configuration: a worker receives the descriptor embedded in the CompiledQueryPlan and constructs its own source instance, with no need to consult the coordinator at runtime.






NoteExample 8.1: descriptor for a TCP source



SourceDescriptor {
    type = "TCP"
    config = {
        "HOST": "vehicle-1.local",
        "PORT": "5001",
        "TIMEOUT_MS": "5000"
    }
    schema = Schema { vehicles$id: UINT64, vehicles$speed: FLOAT64, ... }
    formatter = FormatterDescriptor {
        type = "CSV",
        config = { "TUPLE_DELIMITER": "\n", "FIELD_DELIMITER": "," }
    }
}
Worker receives this descriptor, looks up “TCP” in SourceRegistry, constructs a TCPSource configured with the host/port/timeout, and connects. Same for the formatter.


This is one of the design choices documented in the docs/design/20240702_sources_and_sinks.md design document. The goal stated there is “stateless worker”: all per-source state lives in the descriptor, not on the worker. The motivation is operational: in a future distributed deployment, queries can be moved between workers without state migration; only the descriptor moves.

8.3 8.3 The input formatter chain
Bytes from a source go to an input formatter. The formatter’s job is to read the raw bytes and produce a formatted TupleBuffer whose layout matches the schema.
The formatter splits this into two phases:
Phase 1 — Indexing. Walk the raw bytes, find tuple boundaries, locate field offsets. The output of this phase is a set of indices (byte offsets) into the raw buffer. No data is copied.
Phase 2 — Parsing. Use the indices to read each field’s bytes, convert from the source format (e.g., the ASCII string "3.14") to the native NebulaStream format (the IEEE-754 double value 3.14), and write into the formatted buffer.
The split exists because indexing can sometimes be parallelised (splitting work between threads on tuple boundaries), and because some sources can perform indexing themselves (a Parquet file source already has field offsets), in which case the indexing phase is a no-op.
The interface is in nes-input-formatters/include/InputFormatters/InputFormatter.hpp:
// nes-input-formatters/include/InputFormatters/InputFormatter.hpp
class InputFormatter {
public:
    virtual void parseTupleBuffer(
        const TupleBuffer& rawBuffer,
        TupleBuffer& formattedBuffer,
        const Schema& schema) = 0;
    // ... index-related methods omitted ...
};
The implementations live in nes-input-formatters/:

CSVInputFormatter — handles the textbook case. Configurable field and tuple delimiters.
JSONInputFormatter — uses simdjson (Langdale and Lemire 2019) for fast parsing.
NativeInputFormatter — an identity formatter for sources that already produce native-format tuples.

Adding a formatter is a registry registration plus a class implementing the interface. We will see the registry pattern in Chapter 13.

8.5 8.5 Sinks and output formatters
Sinks mirror sources in shape. The interface is in nes-sinks/include/Sinks/Sink.hpp, smaller because sinks have less work to do:
// nes-sinks/include/Sinks/Sink.hpp
class Sink {
public:
    virtual void start(...) = 0;
    virtual void execute(const TupleBuffer& formatted, ...) = 0;
    virtual void stop(...) = 0;
};
Each execute call receives a formatted TupleBuffer. The sink hands it to its output formatter, which produces the wire-format bytes, then writes those bytes to the destination (file, TCP socket, etc.).
Built-in sinks include FileSink, PrintSink, and (as an optional plugin) VoidSink (for benchmarking — discards everything). Output formatters mirror the input side: CSV and JSON, with a “Native” identity case.
The asymmetry between source and sink declarations in SQL (Chapter 5 mentioned this) is reflected here: sinks combine declaration and implementation in one statement because a single logical sink does not benefit from multiple physical implementations the way a source does.

// nes-query-optimizer/src/QueryOptimizer.cpp
LogicalPlan QueryOptimizer::optimize(
    const LogicalPlan& plan,
    const QueryOptimizerConfiguration& defaultQueryOptimization)
{
    DecideJoinTypesRule joinTypeDecider(defaultQueryOptimization.joinStrategy);
    DecideMemoryLayoutRule memoryLayoutDecider;
    constexpr RedundantUnionRemovalRule redundantUnionRemovalRule{};
    constexpr RedundantProjectionRemovalRule redundantProjectionRemovalRule{};
    constexpr TypeInferenceRule typeInferenceRule{};

    auto optimizedPlan = LogicalPlan{plan};
    redundantProjectionRemovalRule.apply(optimizedPlan);
    redundantUnionRemovalRule.apply(optimizedPlan);
    typeInferenceRule.apply(optimizedPlan);
    optimizedPlan = joinTypeDecider.apply(optimizedPlan);
    optimizedPlan = memoryLayoutDecider.apply(optimizedPlan);
    return optimizedPlan;
}

9.3 9.3 The join-type decision
The most algorithmically interesting rule is DecideJoinTypes. It inspects each JoinLogicalOperator and chooses an implementation type, recording the decision in a JoinImplementationTypeTrait.
The current logic is structural rather than statistical. Look at DecideJoinTypesRule.cpp:
// nes-query-optimizer/src/Rules/Static/DecideJoinTypesRule.cpp
bool shallUseHashJoin(const LogicalFunction& joinFunction)
{
    auto allowedLogicalFunction = [](const LogicalFunction& logicalFunction)
    {
        return logicalFunction.tryGetAs&lt;AndLogicalFunction&gt;().has_value()
            or logicalFunction.tryGetAs&lt;EqualsLogicalFunction&gt;().has_value()
            or logicalFunction.tryGetAs&lt;OrLogicalFunction&gt;().has_value()
            or logicalFunction.tryGetAs&lt;FieldAccessLogicalFunction&gt;().has_value();
    };
    // ... BFS traversal to verify all functions are allowed ...
    return true;  // Default to HashJoin if structure supports it
}
The question the rule answers is not “is hash join faster” but “is hash join applicable.” A hash join requires the predicate to be a conjunction of equalities on field accesses; anything else (a band predicate, an inequality, a UDF) forces the engine to fall back to a nested-loop join.
The rule walks the join’s predicate. If every leaf is a field access and every internal node is AND, OR or =, the rule flags the join for hash. Otherwise nested-loop.
This is a structural decision, not a cost decision. It is also the right decision for the workloads NebulaStream targets: streaming joins almost always are equi-joins on keyed fields, so the rule chooses hash join almost always. The nested-loop fallback exists for safety, not because anyone wants to use it.






NoteExample 9.1: an inequality forces nested-loop



The query
SELECT * FROM trips JOIN tolls
  ON trips.gantry = tolls.gantry AND trips.timestamp BETWEEN tolls.start AND tolls.end
INTO billed_trips;
contains a BETWEEN predicate. The rule sees a LessEquals (or similar inequality) leaf, finds it not in the allowed set, and returns false. The join is tagged for nested-loop execution.
This will be slow on large inputs. A future optimiser would recognise the band-join pattern and use a specialised band-join algorithm (Chapter 22 mentions this as open work).

// nes-physical-operators/include/PhysicalOperator.hpp
template &lt;typename T&gt;
concept PhysicalOperatorConcept = requires(T op) {
    { op.setup(...) };
    { op.open(...) };
    { op.execute(...) };
    { op.close(...) };
};


setup — called once at compile time. Allocates per-operator state, registers handlers.
open — called at the start of each pipeline invocation. Initialises per-invocation state.
execute — called once per Record. The hot path. Most of the actual work happens here.
close — called at the end of each pipeline invocation. Flushes anything pending.

10.3 10.3 The lowering rule registry
Lowering itself is implemented as a rule registry. Each logical operator type has a corresponding lowering rule registered in LoweringRuleRegistry. The rule’s job is to take a logical operator plus context and return a physical operator (or, for stateful operators, a pair).
The dispatch is in LowerToPhysicalOperators.cpp:
// nes-query-compiler/src/Phases/LowerToPhysicalOperators.cpp
std::unique_ptr&lt;AbstractLoweringRule&gt;
resolveLoweringRule(const LogicalOperator& logicalOperator,
                    const LoweringRuleRegistryArguments& registryArgument)
{
    if (logicalOperatorName == "Join")
    {
        const auto implementationTraitOpt
            = getTrait&lt;JoinImplementationTypeTrait&gt;(traitSet);
        switch (implementationTrait-&gt;implementationType)
        {
            case JoinImplementation::HASH_JOIN:
                return LoweringRuleRegistry::instance()
                           .create("HashJoin", registryArgument);
            case JoinImplementation::NESTED_LOOP_JOIN:
                return LoweringRuleRegistry::instance()
                           .create("NLJoin", registryArgument);
        }
    }
    // ... generic case for other operators
}
For a join, the dispatch reads the JoinImplementationTypeTrait attached by DecideJoinTypesRule (Chapter 9) and selects the appropriate physical operator factory by name. For other operators the dispatch is a single-name lookup.
The registry is the same plugin pattern we will see in detail in Chapter 13. Its presence here means a third party can add a new join implementation — say, an interval-join algorithm — by registering a lowering rule named “IntervalJoin” and updating the optimiser’s join-type decision to choose it. The framework is not locked.

10.4 10.4 The hash-join lowering, in detail
A JoinLogicalOperator whose trait says HASH_JOIN lowers into two physical operators:
// nes-physical-operators/include/Join/HashJoin/HJBuildPhysicalOperator.hpp
class HJBuildPhysicalOperator : public StreamJoinBuildPhysicalOperator {
public:
    HJBuildPhysicalOperator(
        OperatorHandlerId operatorHandlerId,
        JoinBuildSideType joinBuildSide,
        std::unique_ptr&lt;TimeFunction&gt; timeFunction,
        const std::shared_ptr&lt;TupleBufferRef&gt;& bufferRef,
        HashMapOptions hashMapOptions);
    void setup(ExecutionContext& executionCtx,
               CompilationContext& compilationContext) const override;
    void execute(ExecutionContext& ctx, Record& record) const override;
};
The build operator accepts records from one side of the join and inserts them into a hash map keyed on the join key. The probe operator accepts records from the other side and looks them up, emitting joined records on each match.
The hash map itself is one of the runtime data structures from nes-nautilus/, a JIT-aware container we will examine in Chapter 12. For now, note that the build’s setup method registers a handler that owns the hash map; both build and probe share access to it through an OperatorHandlerId.
The pair-of-operators pattern is also how the build/probe split introduces a pipeline boundary: the build is on one pipeline, the probe on another, with the hash map mediating between them. That is the Chapter 11 story.

// nes-query-compiler/include/PipelinedQueryPlan.hpp
struct PipelinedQueryPlan final {
    explicit PipelinedQueryPlan(QueryId id, ExecutionMode executionMode);
    [[nodiscard]] std::vector&lt;std::shared_ptr&lt;Pipeline&gt;&gt; getSourcePipelines() const;
    [[nodiscard]] const std::vector&lt;std::shared_ptr&lt;Pipeline&gt;&gt;& getPipelines() const;
    void addPipeline(const std::shared_ptr&lt;Pipeline&gt;& pipeline);
    void removePipeline(Pipeline& pipeline);
};

11.4 11.4 Within a pipeline: the producer/consumer protocol
Inside a pipeline, the operators do not call each other through C++ methods. They communicate through the Record interface, which is the per-tuple data structure the JIT operates on:
// nes-nautilus/include/Nautilus/Interface/Record.hpp
class Record {
public:
    explicit Record() = default;
    explicit Record(std::unordered_map&lt;RecordFieldIdentifier, VarVal&gt;&& recordFields);
    void reassignFields(const Record& other);
    const VarVal& read(const RecordFieldIdentifier& recordFieldIdentifier) const;
    void write(const RecordFieldIdentifier& recordFieldIdentifier,
               const VarVal& varVal);
    nautilus::val&lt;uint64_t&gt; getNumberOfFields() const;
    [[nodiscard]] bool hasField(const RecordFieldIdentifier& fieldName) const;
};
A Record is a typed bag of fields. The fields are of type VarVal, which is a Nautilus-side variant of all the primitive types in NebulaStream’s data model. At code generation time, every Record::read and Record::write is lowered to direct memory accesses (via the MemoryProvider from Chapter 4); there is no runtime hash lookup.
Within a pipeline, the producer’s execute ends with consumer.execute(record); the consumer’s execute reads fields through Record::read, modifies through Record::write, and forwards. The whole chain ends up as a single tight loop in the generated code.

11.5 11.5 The compiled plan
After pipelining and JIT compilation, the plan becomes a CompiledQueryPlan. The structure is in nes-executable/include/CompiledQueryPlan.hpp:
// nes-executable/include/CompiledQueryPlan.hpp
struct CompiledQueryPlan {
    QueryId id;
    std::vector&lt;ExecutablePipelineStage&gt; stages;
    std::vector&lt;SourceDescriptor&gt; sources;
    std::vector&lt;SinkDescriptor&gt; sinks;
    // ...
};
stages is the JIT output: one ExecutablePipelineStage per pipeline, each holding a function pointer (or, in INTERPRETER mode, a trace to interpret). sources and sinks carry the fully-specified descriptors from Chapter 8 — the worker constructs its own source and sink instances from these without consulting the coordinator.
The CompiledQueryPlan is the artefact handed to the query engine in Section 2.6 for execution. It is the boundary between “compile-time” and “run-time”: everything to the left of it is preparation, everything to the right is data flow.

