12.3 12.3 The Nautilus value type: nautilus::val and VarVal
The unit of tracing is nautilus::val&lt;T&gt;, a type from the external Nautilus library. Wherever an operator would naturally use a T — a uint64_t, a bool, a pointer — it uses a nautilus::val&lt;T&gt; instead. Operations on nautilus::val&lt;T&gt; look like operations on T (the type provides the relevant operator overloads), but each operation is recorded into a trace.
Inside NebulaStream, the per-field value type is not nautilus::val&lt;T&gt; directly — it is VarVal, a thin wrapper defined in nes-nautilus/include/Nautilus/DataTypes/VarVal.hpp:
// nes-nautilus/include/Nautilus/DataTypes/VarVal.hpp
template &lt;typename... T&gt;
using var_val_helper =
    std::variant&lt;VariableSizedData, nautilus::val&lt;T&gt;...&gt;;

using var_val_t = var_val_helper&lt;
    bool, uint8_t, uint16_t, uint32_t, uint64_t,
    int8_t, int16_t, int32_t, int64_t,
    float, double, char&gt;;

class VarVal {
    detail::var_val_t value;
    nautilus::val&lt;bool&gt; null;
    bool nullable;
};
VarVal is a std::variant of nautilus::val&lt;T&gt; for every primitive type in NebulaStream’s type system (Chapter 3), plus VariableSizedData for the VARSIZED escape, plus a tracing-aware null flag and a compile-time nullable discriminator. The choice of variant here is deliberate: at trace time, the concrete type of a record field is known (the schema fixes it), so each operator’s read is a std::get&lt;nautilus::val&lt;T&gt;&gt; rather than a runtime type dispatch.
Read a field: record.read("value") returns a VarVal&. Branch on its value: the &gt; threshold comparison is recorded by the underlying nautilus::val&lt;T&gt;::operator&gt;. Assign: output.write("value", v) records a memory write.
To the operator author, the code reads like normal C++:
void SelectionPhysicalOperator::execute(
    ExecutionContext& ctx, Record& record) const
{
    auto v = record.read(fieldName);
    if (v &gt; nautilus::val&lt;uint64_t&gt;(threshold))
    {
        consumer-&gt;execute(ctx, record);
    }
}
To the framework, this is a sequence of trace operations: read, gt-const, if, recursive-execute, endif. The framework captures them, lowers to MLIR, and produces native code that does exactly what the C++ said.

12.5 12.5 The runtime data structures
JIT-friendly data structures matter because the JIT cannot inline through a std::unordered_map’s virtual implementation. Nautilus ships its own.
HashMap. Defined in nes-nautilus/include/Nautilus/Interface/HashMap/HashMap.hpp:
// nes-nautilus/include/Nautilus/Interface/HashMap/HashMap.hpp
class HashMap {
public:
    virtual ~HashMap() = default;
    virtual AbstractHashMapEntry* insertEntry(
        HashFunction::HashValue::raw_type hash,
        AbstractBufferProvider* bufferProvider) = 0;
    [[nodiscard]] virtual uint64_t getNumberOfTuples() const = 0;
};
A small interface. The hash map is laid out across multiple buffers (from the buffer manager), uses linear probing, and exposes a single insertion point that the JIT can inline.
PagedVector. Defined in nes-nautilus/include/Nautilus/Interface/PagedVector/PagedVector.hpp. A dynamically-growing vector backed by a sequence of buffers, with a cumulative-sum index for fast random access:
struct PagedVector {
    struct TupleBufferWithCumulativeSum {
        explicit TupleBufferWithCumulativeSum(TupleBuffer buffer)
            : buffer(std::move(buffer)) { }
        size_t cumulativeSum{0};
        TupleBuffer buffer;
    };
    [[nodiscard]] const TupleBuffer* getTupleBufferForEntry(
        uint64_t entryPos) const;
};
PagedVector is used wherever an operator needs to accumulate an unbounded sequence — the build side of a hash join’s collision chain, an aggregation’s history of tuples per group. Like the hash map, it is structured as a collection of TupleBuffers because that is what fits naturally with the buffer manager.
Record. We met this in Chapter 11. It is the per-tuple data type; its operations (read, write) are the ones operators use in their execute methods.

12.6 12.6 The compile pipeline
The trace-to-native lowering happens in CompiledExecutablePipelineStage (nes-runtime/include/Pipelines/CompiledExecutablePipelineStage.hpp). Its compilePipeline() method registers a lambda with the Nautilus engine; the engine traces the lambda’s execution and produces a callable function pointer:
// nes-runtime/include/Pipelines/CompiledExecutablePipelineStage.cpp (sketch)
nautilus::engine::CallableFunction&lt;void, PipelineExecutionContext*,
                                   const TupleBuffer*, const Arena*&gt;
CompiledExecutablePipelineStage::compilePipeline() const
{
    const std::function&lt;void(
        nautilus::val&lt;PipelineExecutionContext*&gt;,
        nautilus::val&lt;const TupleBuffer*&gt;,
        nautilus::val&lt;const Arena*&gt;)&gt; compiledFunction =
        [this](nautilus::val&lt;PipelineExecutionContext*&gt; pipelineCtx,
               nautilus::val&lt;const TupleBuffer*&gt; recordBufferRef,
               nautilus::val&lt;const Arena*&gt; arenaRef)
    {
        auto ctx = ExecutionContext(pipelineCtx, arenaRef);
        RecordBuffer recordBuffer(recordBufferRef);
        pipeline-&gt;getRootOperator().open(ctx, recordBuffer);
        // ... per-record loop, close, etc. ...
    };
    return engine.registerFunction(compiledFunction);
}
The nautilus::val&lt;…&gt; parameters tell the Nautilus engine which arguments to trace; everything captured by [this] is trace-time-constant. engine.registerFunction is the entry point into the Nautilus library — it does the trace, lowers to MLIR, and returns a callable.
LowerToCompiledQueryPlanPhase walks each pipeline and asks for a compiled stage. The critical configuration:
// nes-query-compiler/src/Phases/LowerToCompiledQueryPlanPhase.cpp
std::unique_ptr&lt;ExecutablePipelineStage&gt;
getStage(const std::shared_ptr&lt;Pipeline&gt;& pipeline)
{
    nautilus::engine::Options options;
    options.setOption("mlir.enableMultithreading", false);
    switch (pipelineQueryPlan-&gt;getExecutionMode())
    {
        case ExecutionMode::COMPILER:
            options.setOption("engine.Compilation", true);
            break;
        case ExecutionMode::INTERPRETER:
            options.setOption("engine.Compilation", false);
            break;
    }
    // ... construct ExecutablePipelineStage with MLIR/Nautilus backend
}
ExecutionMode::COMPILER lowers all the way to native and returns a function pointer. ExecutionMode::INTERPRETER keeps the trace in interpretable form and walks it for each tuple. Both produce the same answer; the latter is much slower but, because the trace is just data, much easier to debug.
The MLIR pipeline is multi-stage: nautilus::ir → mlir-arith → mlir-llvm → LLVM IR → native. Each stage is a lowering pass in the LLVM/MLIR sense (Lattner et al. 2021). The book will not walk through the MLIR specifics; that is a topic large enough for its own book.

14.2 14.2 The Task variant
The full task variant lives in nes-query-engine/Task.hpp. We saw it in Chapter 15:
// nes-query-engine/Task.hpp
using Task = std::variant&lt;
    WorkTask,
    StopQueryTask,
    StartQueryTask,
    FailSourceTask,
    StopSourceTask,
    PendingPipelineStopTask,
    StopPipelineTask,
    StartPipelineTask&gt;;
Eight kinds of task. Two kinds of work: data (WorkTask) and lifecycle (the rest). Worker threads dispatch on the variant via std::visit:
// nes-query-engine/QueryEngine.cpp (sketch)
void handleTask(Task task)
{
    std::visit([&](auto& concrete) {
        runHandler(concrete);
    }, task);
}
Each task type has a dedicated handler. WorkTask handlers call the compiled pipeline’s function pointer with the buffer; lifecycle handlers consult the query catalog and adjust the running state.
The variant pattern means there is no virtual dispatch in the task hierarchy itself — the dispatch is a std::visit jump table — and every task type is type-checked at compile time. Adding a new task type is adding a member to the variant and writing one handler.

14.3 14.3 RunningQueryPlanNode: pipelines as runtime objects
The runtime counterpart of a pipeline is a RunningQueryPlanNode, defined in nes-query-engine/RunningQueryPlan.hpp:
// nes-query-engine/RunningQueryPlan.hpp (sketch)
struct RunningQueryPlanNode {
    PipelineId id;
    std::atomic_bool requiresTermination = false;
    std::atomic&lt;ssize_t&gt; pendingTasks = 0;
    std::vector&lt;std::shared_ptr&lt;RunningQueryPlanNode&gt;&gt; successors;
    std::unique_ptr&lt;ExecutablePipelineStage&gt; stage;
    std::function&lt;void(Exception)&gt; unregisterWithError;
    CallbackRef planRef;
    void fail(Exception exception) const {
        unregisterWithError(std::move(exception));
    }
};
Five fields deserve attention.
pendingTasks is the count of in-flight WorkTasks referring to this pipeline. The PendingPipelineStop machinery from Chapter 15 polls this count to decide when a pipeline can be torn down.
requiresTermination is a flag flipped on after the pipeline’s setup phase completes. It tells the deleter (next paragraph) whether to emit a stop task when the node’s reference count reaches zero. Setup-incomplete nodes can be deleted directly; setup-complete nodes must shut down gracefully.
successors holds shared_ptrs to downstream nodes. This is the engine’s reference graph: a parent node keeps its children alive. When a parent is dropped, the children’s reference count decrements and (potentially) the cascade continues.
stage is the compiled function (Chapter 11), held by unique_ptr because the pipeline node has exclusive ownership.
unregisterWithError is the failure callback, captured at construction. node.fail(exception) invokes it; the callback reports up to the query lifetime listener and tears the query plan down.
The reference-count machinery uses a custom deleter, not C++’s default delete:
// nes-query-engine/RunningQueryPlan.cpp (sketch)
struct RunningQueryPlanNodeDeleter {
    WorkEmitter& emitter;
    QueryId queryId;
    void operator()(RunningQueryPlanNode* node) const noexcept {
        if (node-&gt;requiresTermination.load()) {
            emitter.emitPipelineStop(queryId, std::unique_ptr&lt;RunningQueryPlanNode&gt;(node));
        } else {
            delete node;
        }
    }
};

std::shared_ptr&lt;RunningQueryPlanNode&gt; RunningQueryPlanNode::create(
    QueryId queryId, PipelineId pipelineId, WorkEmitter& emitter,
    std::vector&lt;std::shared_ptr&lt;RunningQueryPlanNode&gt;&gt; successors,
    std::unique_ptr&lt;ExecutablePipelineStage&gt; stage,
    std::function&lt;void(Exception)&gt; unregisterWithError,
    CallbackRef planRef, CallbackRef setupCallback)
{
    auto node = std::shared_ptr&lt;RunningQueryPlanNode&gt;(
        new RunningQueryPlanNode(pipelineId, std::move(successors),
                                 std::move(stage), std::move(unregisterWithError),
                                 std::move(planRef)),
        RunningQueryPlanNodeDeleter{.emitter = emitter, .queryId = queryId});
    emitter.emitPipelineStart(queryId, node, std::move(setupCallback));
    return node;
}
When the last shared_ptr to a node is dropped, the deleter runs. If the node’s setup completed (requiresTermination == true), the deleter emits a PipelineStop task and transfers ownership of the raw pointer into a unique_ptr inside the task. The pipeline gets torn down asynchronously, on a worker thread, through the same task queue that processes data.
Tasks reference pipelines through weak_ptr. A worker about to process a WorkTask first locks the weak_ptr to a shared_ptr; if the lock fails (the pipeline has been destroyed under us), the task is dropped. This is the engine’s primary defence against use-after-free across worker threads, and it is the reason the custom deleter must transfer ownership through a task rather than tearing down inline — by the time the deleter runs, other worker threads may still hold weak_ptrs that they expect to locate cleanly.

14.5 14.5 The NodeEngine: the bridge between compiled plan and runtime
A small but important module we have not yet pinned down: nes-runtime/. It provides the NodeEngine class — the single object that owns the per-worker runtime context. Its job is to bridge from the compile pipeline’s output (a CompiledQueryPlan) to the worker’s execution machinery (the two-queue scheduler, the buffer pool, the source registry).
Concretely, NodeEngine exposes:
// nes-runtime/include/NodeEngine.hpp (sketch)
class NodeEngine {
public:
    void registerQuery(std::unique_ptr&lt;CompiledQueryPlan&gt; plan);
    void startQuery(QueryId id);
    void stopQuery(QueryId id);
    QueryStatus queryStatus(QueryId id) const;
    // ...
};
This is also where the nes-executable/ module’s CompiledQueryPlan lives, structurally:
// nes-executable/include/CompiledQueryPlan.hpp (sketch)
struct CompiledQueryPlan {
    QueryId id;
    std::vector&lt;std::shared_ptr&lt;ExecutablePipeline&gt;&gt; pipelines;
    std::vector&lt;SourceDescriptor&gt; sources;
    std::vector&lt;SinkDescriptor&gt; sinks;
};

class ExecutablePipeline {
    PipelineId id;
    std::unique_ptr&lt;ExecutablePipelineStage&gt; stage;
    std::vector&lt;std::shared_ptr&lt;ExecutablePipeline&gt;&gt; successors;
};
The ExecutablePipelineStage is the abstract interface that the JIT (Chapter 12) implements; CompiledExecutablePipelineStage is the concrete subclass holding the function pointer. PipelineExecutionContext (also in nes-runtime/) carries the per-pipeline runtime state — buffer providers, listeners — that operators consult during execution.
The takeaway: nes-executable/ defines the shape of a compiled query; nes-runtime/ defines the engine that runs it. The query engine module (nes-query-engine/) holds the scheduler that schedules its tasks. The three modules are distinct because their lifetimes are different: a CompiledQueryPlan outlives any individual run; the NodeEngine lives for the worker’s lifetime; the query engine’s schedulable tasks come and go per buffer.

15.2 15.2 Solving P1: do not block the worker on emit
The cleanest fix to P1 is to ensure that worker threads never block on emit. A worker thread that holds work must always be able to make progress.
NebulaStream’s published design document (NebulaStream contributors 2024, §Continuation) proposes a technique called continuation. When a worker fails to write its follow-up task to the queue, instead of blocking, the worker swaps: it stuffs its unsubmittable task into a thread-local slot and pulls one task off the queue to work on next. The next time the worker comes back to its slot, the queue may have drained enough to accept the swapped task.
Continuation is elegant. It also has a subtle trap, identified in the same design document: some tasks cannot make progress on their own. A PendingPipelineStop task — which we will examine in detail in Section 15.7 — checks whether a pipeline still has references and, if so, re-emits itself. If continuation forced the worker to take this task off the queue and immediately swap it back, the worker would loop without making progress.
The published design proposes a workaround: instead of swapping, the worker takes one task off the queue, replaces its own task with that one, and works on the replacement. The original task gets put back at the end of the queue.






WarningCaveat: the live code uses a different fix



The design document was written when the internal queue was bounded. The current implementation uses an unbounded internal queue. The worker-emit path therefore cannot fail at all; continuation is no longer needed for P1.
You can verify this directly. Open /Users/zeuchste/git/nebulastream/nes-query-engine/TaskQueue.hpp and observe that the internal queue is declared as folly::UMPMCQueue&lt;TaskType, true&gt; — the U is for unbounded — while only the admission queue is folly::MPMCQueue&lt;TaskType&gt;. The method addInternalTaskNonBlocking carries this guarantee in its name, and its body has no failure path:
template &lt;typename T = TaskType&gt;
void addInternalTaskNonBlocking(T&& task)
{
    /// The order of operation upholds the invariant. internal is
    /// unbounded which makes this write always succeed (unless oom)
    internal.enqueue(std::forward&lt;T&gt;(task));
    tasksAvailable.release();
}
This is the engine’s actual P1 fix as of cd3ef68c73: don’t bound the queue worker threads write to. We will use this as our reference throughout the chapter and note where the design document’s continuation scheme would still be needed if a future change made the internal queue bounded again.


The choice between an unbounded internal queue and a bounded queue plus continuation is a real engineering trade-off, not a settled matter.
The unbounded approach buys simplicity. Worker code never needs to handle a write failure; emission is a trivial enqueue. It also buys correctness for the PendingPipelineStop scenario: a self-emitting task can simply be appended without any concern about replacing a worker’s current task.
The cost is that the engine no longer has a hard backpressure surface on the worker side. If a query produces a pathological amount of internal fan-out — imagine a many-to-many join with no selectivity — the internal queue can grow until it crashes the process. In practice this is bounded by other constraints: the number of TupleBuffers in the pool (we will see why in Chapter 16), the selectivity of upstream operators, the rate at which sources can be admitted (the next section). NebulaStream’s authors have decided that those constraints are sufficient. Other engines have decided differently; we will see Flink’s choice in Section 15.4’s sidebar.

15.3 15.3 Solving P2: a separate admission queue
P2 — source-flooding — needs a different fix because it has a different cause. The issue is not that producers can block; it is that some kinds of producers should be allowed to block, and others should not. Sources can wait. Worker threads cannot.
NebulaStream’s solution is to physically separate the two kinds of producers into two queues:

internal — the unbounded queue that worker threads emit into. Always accepts a write; never blocks.
admission — a bounded queue that sources, query starts, and query stops emit into. Blocks the producer when full.

Worker threads dequeue from a logical merger of both: the internal queue is preferred, the admission queue is fallback. From the worker’s point of view, there is just one source of work; from the producer’s point of view, there are two clearly distinct paths with different blocking semantics.
The implementation is in nes-query-engine/TaskQueue.hpp. Two folly queues, one semaphore, three public methods. The whole file is 124 lines.
// nes-query-engine/TaskQueue.hpp:34-44
template &lt;typename TaskType&gt;
class TaskQueue
{
    folly::UMPMCQueue&lt;TaskType, true&gt; internal;
    folly::MPMCQueue&lt;TaskType&gt; admission;

    /// INVARIANT: internal.size() + admission.size() &gt;= tasksAvailable
    std::counting_semaphore&lt;&gt; tasksAvailable{0};
    // ...
};
The line INVARIANT: internal.size() + admission.size() &gt;= tasksAvailable is the heart of the design. We will return to it in Section 15.5.
Three public write/read methods complete the interface:
// nes-query-engine/TaskQueue.hpp:71-94
template &lt;typename T = TaskType&gt;
bool addAdmissionTaskBlocking(const std::stop_token& stoken, T&& task);

template &lt;typename T = TaskType&gt;
void addInternalTaskNonBlocking(T&& task);

std::optional&lt;TaskType&gt; getNextTaskBlocking(const std::stop_token& stoken);
Three methods, three intents. The method names carry the semantics: admission is blocking, internal is non-blocking, dequeue is blocking with cancellation. There is also a non-blocking dequeue (getNextTaskNonBlocking) which we will not need in this chapter.






NoteExample 15.2: P2 in a fast source



A TCPSource is connected to a sensor producing a million events per second. Each batch of ten thousand events becomes one WorkTask written into the admission queue. The admission queue is sized at, say, four hundred slots.
If the workers can process eight hundred batches per second, the queue fills in half a second. The source thread blocks on addAdmissionTaskBlocking. Meanwhile, the workers continue to drain — and crucially, when a worker emits a follow-up task into the internal queue, that emission still succeeds, because the internal queue is unbounded.
The source’s blocking has, by design, no effect on worker progress. The fast sensor experiences backpressure (its TCP buffer fills, its TCP window shrinks, the upstream sensor learns to slow down) but the engine is not deadlocked. P2 is solved.

15.4 15.4 Reading order: who wins the dequeue race
We have two queues. A worker thread asks for the next task. Which queue do we read from?
The simple rule: prefer internal, fall back to admission.
// nes-query-engine/TaskQueue.hpp:47-63
TaskType readElementAssumingItExists()
{
    TaskType task;
    /// The semaphore guarantees that there is at least one element in
    /// either one of the queues.
    if (internal.try_dequeue(task))
    {
        return task;
    }

    /// However, the MPMC `read` can spuriously fail under high contention,
    /// the alternative `readIfNotEmpty` does not but is significantly
    /// slower.
    while (!admission.read(task)) [[unlikely]]
    {
    }

    return task;
}
The internal queue is checked first because it represents work already accepted into the engine. Tuples currently passing between operators have priority over new tuples knocking at the door. This is the heart of the deadlock avoidance: a worker that cannot emit is, by construction, impossible (the internal queue is unbounded), but if it could be impossible, we would still want this read order, because it ensures that work-in-progress drains before new work is admitted.
Said differently: this discipline gives priority to old work. If the admission queue is overwhelmed, sources will block. If the internal queue is overwhelmed, the workers will eventually drain it because nothing fights them for it.






TipReal-system reference: how this differs in Flink



Apache Flink uses a different model. Each operator has a fixed pool of network buffers (NetworkBufferPool) (Hueske and Kalavri 2019). When an upstream operator emits a record, it must obtain a buffer; if no buffers are available, it blocks. There is no separate “admission” queue: backpressure travels through the operator chain itself, and the source naturally blocks because everything between it and the source has filled up.
Flink’s approach has the virtue of fine-grained, per-pipeline backpressure (one slow operator backpressures only its predecessors) but is intrinsically more complex: every operator has a buffer budget, every emit can block, every operator must be able to correctly handle blocking on emit.
NebulaStream’s two-queue model is coarser. Backpressure is global to the engine, not per-operator. The simplification is the point.


There is one subtle implementation note in readElementAssumingItExists that deserves attention: the while loop on admission.read. The loop is annotated [[unlikely]] and a comment explains why: the folly MPMCQueue::read method can spuriously fail under high contention even when the queue is non-empty. The semaphore promises us a task is available; the queue, briefly, lies about it. The alternative readIfNotEmpty does not lie but is — the comment tells us — significantly slower.
This is a textbook example of the kind of trade-off you do not see unless you read the actual code. A design document, when summarising, will say “we read from the queue.” The implementation says “we sometimes spin on a contended dequeue because the alternative is too slow.” Both are true; the second is more useful.

15.7 15.7 Pending pipeline stops: when even two queues are not enough
Stopping a pipeline gracefully means waiting until no task currently references it. Tasks reference pipelines through a weak_ptr; the pipeline node is shared_ptr-owned by the engine, with task-local weak_ptr references. Until the last task referencing a pipeline has run to completion, the pipeline cannot be torn down without risking a use-after-free.
The engine therefore models graceful pipeline shutdown as a polling task: a PendingPipelineStopTask that examines the pipeline’s reference count, and if any task is still referencing it, re-emits itself to be checked again later.
This is the task type that broke the simple version of continuation. A PendingPipelineStopTask cannot make progress on its own; it can only make progress as other, unrelated tasks ahead of it in the queue finish.
The current implementation handles this with a small backoff mechanism. Look at QueryEngine.cpp:600-625:
// nes-query-engine/QueryEngine.cpp:608-624
if (pendingPipelineStop.pipeline-&gt;pendingTasks &gt; 0)
{
    ENGINE_LOG_TRACE(
        "Pipeline {}-{} is still active: {}. Seen for {}th time",
        pendingPipelineStop.queryId,
        pendingPipelineStop.pipeline-&gt;id,
        pendingPipelineStop.pipeline-&gt;pendingTasks,
        pendingPipelineStop.attempts);

    PendingPipelineStopTask repeatTask(
        pendingPipelineStop.queryId,
        pendingPipelineStop.pipeline,
        pendingPipelineStop.attempts + 1,
        std::move(pendingPipelineStop.callback));
    if (pendingPipelineStop.attempts &gt;= PIPELINE_STOP_BACKOFF_THRESHOLD)
    {
        pool.delayedTaskSubmitter.submitTaskIn(
            std::move(repeatTask), PIPELINE_STOP_BACKOFF_INTERVAL);
    }
    else
    {
        pool.addInternalTask(std::move(repeatTask));
    }
    return false;
}
The pattern is: try the immediate re-enqueue twice; on the third attempt, route through a DelayedTaskSubmitter that holds the task for PIPELINE_STOP_BACKOFF_INTERVAL (25 ms, defined two screens up at line 70) before re-introducing it. The constants are deliberately small. This is not exponential backoff. It is a single hop from “try again immediately” to “try again in 25 ms” — enough delay to let the admission queue drain a few tasks but not enough to noticeably affect shutdown latency.






NoteExample 15.4: walking through a pipeline stop



A pipeline P has three in-flight WorkTasks. A StopQueryTask for P’s query arrives; the engine processes it by emitting a PendingPipelineStopTask for P.
The PendingPipelineStopTask is dequeued. Worker observes pipeline-&gt;pendingTasks == 3, increments attempts to 1, and re- emits the task to the internal queue. The task is now behind two remaining WorkTasks.
Worker dequeues a WorkTask, processes it, decrements pendingTasks to 2. Then dequeues the PendingPipelineStopTask again. Sees 2 remaining, increments attempts to 2, re-emits.
Now attempts == 2 == PIPELINE_STOP_BACKOFF_THRESHOLD. The task is routed through DelayedTaskSubmitter and held for 25 ms. During that window, the two remaining WorkTasks drain. When the delayed task is reinjected, pendingTasks == 0, the pipeline is torn down, the callback fires.


A natural question: why a backoff at all? Why not let the PendingPipelineStopTask repeatedly re-enqueue itself until the references drop to zero?
The answer is in the comment immediately above the threshold check (QueryEngine.cpp:614-615):

If we have seen this pipeline for the third time, we will add some work from the admission queue to the internal queue. We need to do this, as the pipeline might be stuck in a deadlock as it is waiting for data from a source which has not been moved into the internal queue.

A pipeline that is waiting for data from a source can only get that data via the admission queue. If the worker keeps spinning on PendingPipelineStopTask re-enqueues, no admission-queue task will ever advance, because the internal queue keeps refilling itself with the same self-emitting task. The 25 ms delay is not just polite; it is correctness. It guarantees that the worker’s gaze leaves the internal queue long enough for a source-emitted task to be promoted.
This is a third deadlock possibility — a sub-case of P1 — that the two-queue design alone does not solve. It needs the DelayedTaskSubmitter as a dedicated escape hatch.

15.8 15.8 Cancellation as a first-class concern
The two queues block in different ways. The admission queue blocks producers when it is full. The dequeue blocks consumers when both queues are empty. Either blocking call must be cancellable — the engine has to be able to shut down even if every queue is in a pathological state.
NebulaStream uses C++20’s std::stop_token for this, plumbed through both blocking methods. Look at the dequeue path:
// nes-query-engine/TaskQueue.hpp:100-111
std::optional&lt;TaskType&gt; getNextTaskBlocking(const std::stop_token& stoken)
{
    while (!tasksAvailable.try_acquire_for(StopTokenCheckInterval))
    {
        if (stoken.stop_requested())
        {
            return std::nullopt;
        }
    }

    return readElementAssumingItExists();
}
The semaphore is acquired with a timeout — StopTokenCheckInterval, defined as 100 ms. Each timeout, the thread checks the stop token. If shutdown has been requested, the method returns an empty optional and the worker exits. Otherwise, it tries to acquire again.
The 100 ms interval is the maximum cancellation latency a worker thread experiences when it is waiting for work. It is short enough that a user-initiated shutdown feels instant. It is long enough that the polling cost is invisible: ten checks per second per thread is nothing.
The same pattern guards the producer side. addAdmissionTaskBlocking loops tryWriteUntil with the same 100 ms interval, returning false if the stop token fires before the write succeeds.
// nes-query-engine/TaskQueue.hpp:71-85
template &lt;typename T = TaskType&gt;
bool addAdmissionTaskBlocking(const std::stop_token& stoken, T&& task)
{
    while (!stoken.stop_requested())
    {
        if (admission.tryWriteUntil(
                std::chrono::steady_clock::now() + StopTokenCheckInterval,
                std::forward&lt;T&gt;(task)))
        {
            tasksAvailable.release();
            return true;
        }
    }
    return false;
}
There is one piece of nuance worth remarking on. The dequeue method’s documentation states: “The method prioritizes reading over cancellation. This implies, if a read is non-blocking, it succeeds regardless of the state of the stop token.” That is, a worker that finds work available will process it even during shutdown, rather than exiting with the work unfinished. This is a design choice in favour of draining over abandonment. It pairs naturally with the PendingPipelineStopTask mechanism: shutdown processes shut down gracefully through the same queue, alongside the data they are tearing down.






TipReal-system reference: Spark Streaming and graceful stop



Spark Structured Streaming similarly distinguishes “stop now” from “stop gracefully” via the gracefulStop parameter on StreamingQuery.stop() (Armbrust et al. 2018). The mechanism differs — Spark drains the current micro-batch and then halts — but the underlying principle is the same: producers and consumers both need a cooperative cancellation signal, and “drain in flight, then exit” is almost always the right default.
NebulaStream’s choice to model graceful stop as a first-class PendingPipelineStopTask traveling through the worker queue (rather than as out-of-band lifecycle state) makes the draining semantics natural to enforce.

16.2 16.2 The hash join’s state
For a hash join, the build operator inserts each input tuple into the hash map keyed on the join key. Hash collisions chain at the bucket head. The probe operator looks up incoming tuples by hash and walks the chain emitting matches.
The interface is small:
// nes-nautilus/include/Nautilus/Interface/HashMap/HashMap.hpp
class HashMap {
public:
    virtual ~HashMap() = default;
    virtual AbstractHashMapEntry* insertEntry(
        HashFunction::HashValue::raw_type hash,
        AbstractBufferProvider* bufferProvider) = 0;
    [[nodiscard]] virtual uint64_t getNumberOfTuples() const = 0;
};
The concrete implementation is ChainedHashMap, in nes-nautilus/src/Nautilus/Interface/HashMap/ChainedHashMap/ChainedHashMap.cpp. The insertion path is worth reading in full because it shows how the hash map’s storage is actually paged across TupleBuffers:
// nes-nautilus/src/Nautilus/Interface/HashMap/ChainedHashMap/ChainedHashMap.cpp (sketch)
AbstractHashMapEntry* ChainedHashMap::insertEntry(
    const HashFunction::HashValue::raw_type hash,
    AbstractBufferProvider* bufferProvider)
{
    // Step 0: lazy bucket-array initialisation
    if (entries == nullptr) [[unlikely]] {
        const auto totalSpace = (numberOfChains + 1) * sizeof(ChainedHashMapEntry*);
        entryBuffer = bufferProvider-&gt;getUnpooledBuffer(totalSpace);
        entries = reinterpret_cast&lt;ChainedHashMapEntry**&gt;(
            entryBuffer.getAvailableMemoryArea().data());
        std::memset(entries, 0, entryBuffer-&gt;getBufferSize());
    }
    // Step 1: allocate a new storage page if the current one is full
    if (numberOfTuples % entriesPerPage == 0) {
        storageSpace.emplace_back(bufferProvider-&gt;getUnpooledBuffer(pageSize).value());
    }
    // Step 2: locate the next entry slot in the current page
    const auto pageIndex = numberOfTuples / entriesPerPage;
    auto& bufferStorage = storageSpace[pageIndex];
    const auto entryOffset =
        (numberOfTuples - pageIndex * entriesPerPage) * entrySize;
    // Step 3: chain at bucket head (head of bucket's linked list)
    const auto entryPos = hash & mask;
    auto* const newEntry = new (
        bufferStorage.getAvailableMemoryArea().subspan(entryOffset).data())
        ChainedHashMapEntry(hash);
    newEntry-&gt;next = entries[entryPos];
    entries[entryPos] = newEntry;
    ++numberOfTuples;
    return newEntry;
}
Four observations.
The bucket array is allocated lazily. First insertion allocates (numberOfChains + 1) * sizeof(pointer) bytes via an unpooled buffer (sized for the array, not the standard 8 KiB). Empty hash maps cost nothing.
Entries are stored in a sequence of pages. Each page is one TupleBuffer from the pool; once full, a new buffer is allocated. This is what “paged across TupleBuffers” means in practice — the hash map is not one contiguous arena, it is a vector of pages, and the buffer pool is the allocator.
Insertion is at the head of the bucket chain. Newly- inserted entries are visited first by probes. For a join where the most recently inserted records are the most likely matches, this is faster than tail insertion.
The mask is a power-of-two AND. hash & mask requires mask = numberOfChains - 1 and numberOfChains to be a power of two. This is faster than modulo and standard for in-memory hash tables; the cost is that the chain count must round up to a power of two.






NoteExample 16.1: a hash join in flight



For SELECT * FROM r JOIN s ON r.k = s.k INTO matched, the build side accumulates r’s tuples until r’s watermark advances past the join window’s end. Suppose r has 100,000 tuples; the hash map holds 100,000 entries, paged across roughly 30 buffers (each holding ~3,300 entries depending on entry size).
When s’s tuples arrive, the probe operator hashes each, jumps to the entry, walks the collision chain (typically one or two entries per bucket if the hash function is good), and emits one output tuple per match. After the join window closes, the operator handler tears down the hash map, releasing 30 buffers back to the pool.

16.4 16.4 The slice abstraction
Window state has a finite lifetime: it lives until the window fires, plus a grace period for late data, then it can be released. The engine’s mechanism for this is the slice.
A slice is a contiguous time range of state. For tumbling windows of size 1 second, every slice is also 1 second. For sliding windows of size 5 seconds, advancing every 1 second, the slice boundary is the GCD of the size and advance — 1 second. The mechanic is in WindowSlicesStoreInterface (in nes-physical-operators/include/).
When a tuple arrives with event-time timestamp t, it is assigned to a slice. Each slice has an end time t_end; when the engine’s notion of progress crosses t_end, the slice can be probed (its windows fire) and, after the probe, garbage collected.
The handler is WindowBasedOperatorHandler, defined in nes-physical-operators/include/:
// nes-physical-operators/include/WindowBasedOperatorHandler.hpp (sketch)
class WindowBasedOperatorHandler : public OperatorHandler {
    std::unique_ptr&lt;WindowSlicesStoreInterface&gt; sliceAndWindowStore;
    std::unique_ptr&lt;MultiOriginWatermarkProcessor&gt; watermarkProcessorBuild;
    std::unique_ptr&lt;MultiOriginWatermarkProcessor&gt; watermarkProcessorProbe;
public:
    WindowBasedOperatorHandler(
        const std::vector&lt;OriginId&gt;& inputOrigins,
        const OriginId outputOriginId,
        std::unique_ptr&lt;WindowSlicesStoreInterface&gt; sliceAndWindowStore);
    void setWorkerThreads(uint64_t numberOfWorkerThreads);
    void checkAndTriggerWindows(const BufferMetaData&, PipelineExecutionContext*);
    virtual void triggerSlices(
        const std::map&lt;WindowInfoAndSequenceNumber,
                       std::vector&lt;std::shared_ptr&lt;Slice&gt;&gt;&gt;&,
        PipelineExecutionContext*) = 0;
};
The handler holds two MultiOriginWatermarkProcessors: watermarkProcessorBuild for input origins and watermarkProcessorProbe for the single output origin (the output of the operator becomes a new origin in its own right — see Chapter 7). The constructor wires them up:
// nes-physical-operators/src/WindowBasedOperatorHandler.cpp (sketch)
WindowBasedOperatorHandler::WindowBasedOperatorHandler(
    const std::vector&lt;OriginId&gt;& inputOrigins,
    const OriginId outputOriginId,
    std::unique_ptr&lt;WindowSlicesStoreInterface&gt; sliceAndWindowStore)
    : sliceAndWindowStore(std::move(sliceAndWindowStore)),
      watermarkProcessorBuild(
          std::make_unique&lt;MultiOriginWatermarkProcessor&gt;(inputOrigins)),
      watermarkProcessorProbe(
          std::make_unique&lt;MultiOriginWatermarkProcessor&gt;(
              std::vector{outputOriginId}))
{}
checkAndTriggerWindows is called as buffers arrive and watermarks advance. It examines the build watermark, identifies any slices whose end time has been crossed, and calls triggerSlices for them. triggerSlices is operator-specific — for an aggregation, it walks the per-slice hash maps and emits one tuple per group.
Each window slice carries a hash map per worker thread:
// nes-physical-operators/include/Aggregation/AggregationSlice.hpp (sketch)
class AggregationSlice final : public HashMapSlice {
public:
    HashMap* getHashMapPtr(WorkerThreadId workerThreadId) const;
    HashMap* getHashMapPtrOrCreate(WorkerThreadId workerThreadId);
};
Per-thread hash maps avoid contention on the hot path: each worker writes into its own shard. At trigger time, the probe phase merges shards before emitting results. This is a classical sharding-vs-locking trade-off, and the code chose sharding.
A small state-machine governs each window’s lifetime: WINDOW_FILLING → EMITTED_TO_PROBE (or WAITING_ON_TERMINATION on shutdown). The store advances windows through these states based on watermark progress; once emitted, the slice’s storage is eligible for garbage collection.

17.5 17.5 Recoverable errors: the std::expected pattern
Some errors are not exceptional in the “should never happen” sense — they are routine. The user submits a query that fails to parse. A source’s file path is wrong. The right response is not “throw and unwind”, it is “return the error to the caller.”
NebulaStream uses std::expected&lt;T, Exception&gt; for these cases. The query-registration path returns one:
// nes-single-node-worker/src/SingleNodeWorker.cpp (sketch)
std::expected&lt;QueryId, Exception&gt;
SingleNodeWorker::registerQuery(LogicalPlan plan) noexcept
{
    if (plan.getQueryId() == INVALID_QUERY_ID) {
        std::uniform_int_distribution&lt;size_t&gt; dist(
            QueryId::INITIAL,
            std::numeric_limits&lt;int32_t&gt;::max());
        plan.setQueryId(QueryId(dist(*idGenerator.wlock())));
    }
    auto request = std::make_unique&lt;QueryCompilation::QueryCompilationRequest&gt;(plan);
    auto result = compiler-&gt;compileQuery(std::move(request));
    // returns std::expected&lt;QueryId, Exception&gt;
}
The function is noexcept and returns either a QueryId (success) or an Exception (a typed error from the exception list). The caller decides how to respond — typically by translating the exception into a gRPC status code for the client.
This pattern keeps the normal flow visible — the function signature reads “either I return a QueryId or I report an error” — while keeping bugs (which use exceptions or terminate) clearly distinct.

Reads its configuration (file path or CLI args).
Constructs a BufferManager, an ExecutableQueryPlan compiler, a QueryEngine, and the catalogs.
Calls registerAll on each plugin registry to populate them.
Starts the gRPC server on (default) localhost:8080.
Waits for queries.


